						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-891087/help:questions-desk">Writing Help / Help: Questions Desk</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13960765/scp-instance-naming">SCP instance naming</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4854495">
									

<blockquote>
<p>so what exactly is this for?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Basically any sort of specific thing that is related to a primary anomaly, but distinct enough to make more sense to mention specifically. So if the Foundation was containing an anomalous field and there was an anomalous rabbit that only shows up in that field, the field would be SCP-XXXX and the rabbit would be SCP-XXXX-1. That helps prevent confusion/strange sentences where it would make sense to refer to only one thing at a time (for example, &quot;SCP-XXXX-1 consumes solely dandelion greens&quot; makes sense for the rabbit, not the field).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>additionally I'm wondering if something like SCP-XXXX-1-1 would be acceptable or if it should just be referred to as SCP-XXXX-2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It depends on what relationship that something has to SCP-XXXX-1. You would use an offshoot that has an existing designation offshoot number if you're referring to a specific part of that previously designated thing. To use the previous example, if the rabbit carried a good luck charm that had its own anomalous properties, that might be SCP-XXXX-1-1, though I think most authors would use SCP-XXXX-1-A or SCP-XXXX-1a or the like.</p>
<p>So basically:</p>
<ul>
<li>SCP-XXXX: main anomaly/main collection of anomalies the Foundation is containing</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX-1: part of/a component of SCP-XXXX</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX-1-A: part of/a component of SCP-XXXX-1</li>
<li>and so on.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
